    role: 'Queen Mother of the Zulu Nation',
    years: 'c. 1760 – 1827',
    image: '/images/maasai-women2.jpg',
    description: 'The mother of Shaka Zulu, Nandi endured exile and hardship before her son rose to power. She became the most powerful woman in the Zulu kingdom.',
    fullBio: `Nandi was a woman of extraordinary resilience, dignity, and strength whose life story is inseparable from the rise of the Zulu nation. Born into the Langeni clan, she became pregnant by the Zulu chief Senzangakhona under circumstances that violated customary law, and was rejected and humiliated by the Zulu community. She raised her son Shaka alone, moving between clans in poverty and disgrace, enduring years of exile and social rejection. Through all of this she instilled in Shaka a fierce pride, a burning ambition, and an unshakeable sense of identity. When Shaka seized power and built his kingdom, he elevated his mother to the highest position in the land — granting her the title of Queen Mother and surrounding her with honour and ceremony that reflected the depth of his devotion. Her death in 1827 shattered Shaka so completely that he declared a period of national mourning of devastating severity — ordering that no crops be planted and no cattle milked for a year. Nandi is remembered as the woman whose love, sacrifice, and suffering gave the Zulu nation its greatest king.`,
    quote: 'A mother\'s love is the foundation upon which kingdoms are built.',
    quoteContext: 'Attributed to Nandi, Queen Mother of the Zulu Nation',
