    years: '1938 – 1997',
    image: '/images/drums-latest.jpg',
    description: 'Creator of Afrobeat music and one of Africa\'s most defiant voices against government corruption, Fela Kuti turned his Lagos compound into a republic called "Kalakuta."',
    fullBio: `Fela Anikulapo Kuti was the most defiant, most original, and most politically dangerous musician Africa has ever produced. Born into a prominent Yoruba family in Abeokuta — his mother Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti was a legendary feminist activist — Fela studied music in London before travelling to the United States where his encounter with the Black Power movement radicalized him permanently. Returning to Lagos, he fused Yoruba music, jazz, funk, and political commentary into a new genre he called Afrobeat — hypnotic, hour-long compositions played by his band Africa 70 that filled Lagos nightclubs and shook the Nigerian government to its core. His compound in Lagos, which he declared the independent Kalakuta Republic, became a sanctuary for the poor, the dispossessed, and the rebellious. The Nigerian military raided Kalakuta twice — on the second raid in 1977, over a thousand soldiers destroyed the compound and threw his 78-year-old mother from a window. She died from her injuries. Fela responded by delivering her coffin to the military barracks. He was arrested over 200 times but never stopped playing or speaking. He died of AIDS-related complications in 1997, and over a million people attended his funeral in Lagos.`,
