    fullBio: `Sir Ahmadu Bello, the Sardauna of Sokoto, was the most powerful and influential political figure in northern Nigeria and one of the founding fathers of the Nigerian state. Born in Rabbah in what is now Sokoto State, he was a great-grandson of the legendary Islamic reformer Usman dan Fodio, and inherited both his ancestor's religious authority and his commitment to just governance. Educated at the Katsina College — the premier school for the sons of northern Nigerian nobility — he became a teacher and district scribe before entering politics. As the leader of the Northern People's Congress and the first and only Premier of the Northern Region of Nigeria, he governed a territory larger than most African countries with a population of over 30 million people. He was a moderniser who built schools, hospitals, and roads across the north while carefully preserving Islamic traditions and the emirate system. He prioritised the education of northerners who had been left behind by the British colonial system, sending thousands of students abroad for training. He was deeply committed to Nigerian unity and worked to build bridges between the Muslim north and the Christian south. On January 15, 1966, he was assassinated in the first Nigerian military coup, along with Prime Minister Tafawa Balewa — a tragedy that set Nigeria on the path to civil war. He remains the most revered political figure in northern Nigeria.`,
